                       COMPLETE OPTIMIZATION HISTORY                    

==============================================================================

1. SD Card Initalization Order - CRITICAL

Problem: White screen crashes, and SD failures
Root Cause: Bluepad32 BT stack allocates ~47KB before setup() runs, fragmenting heap.
SD.begin() needs contiguous blocks for FAT buffers.

Solution:

// CORRECT BOOT ORDER:
void setup() {
  // 1. Malloc ALL large buffers FIRST (before heap fragments)
  fbuf = (uint16_t*)malloc(GB_W * GB_H * 2);
  bank0Cache = (uint8_t*)malloc(ROMBANK);
  bank1Cache = (uint8_t*)malloc(ROMBANK);
  cram = (uint8_t*)malloc(CRAM);
  gbp = (struct gb_s*)malloc(sizeof(struct gb_s));
  lbuf = (uint16_t*)malloc(GB_W * 2 * 2);
  
  // 2. SD.begin() SECOND (gets remaining contiguous blocks)
  SD.begin(SD_CS, sdspi, 400000);  // Slow init
  SD.end();
  SD.begin(SD_CS, sdspi, 25000000); // Renegotiate to 25MHz
  
  // 3. TFT.init() THIRD
  tft.init();
  
  // 4. Bluepad32 LAST (gets fragmented leftovers)
  BP32.setup(&onConn, &onDisc);
}

Why This Works:
  - Largest allocations happen when heap is pristine (no fragmentation)
  - SD FAT LIBRARY can find contigous blocks in middle of heap
  - Display init doesn't conflict with SD state machine

Technical Detail: 
After SW_CPU_RESET, Bluepad32 reinitalizes BT before setup() runs (in ardunios main()),
leaving only  ~56KB instead of ~103KB. Allocating early captures the large block before
BT fragments it.

